    "elevatorPitch": "Most negotiations stall because people dig into stubborn trenches like two drivers refusing to yield in a narrow alleyway. Principled negotiation unlocks agreement by attacking the underlying problem together rather than attacking each other.",
    "executiveSummary": "Think of typical bargaining like haggling at an open market: each party plants an arbitrary flag in the ground and refuses to budge. Fisher and Ury expose the hidden mechanics of this deadlock. When we defend an entrenched position, our ego fuses to our demand, making any concession feel like personal defeat. Principled negotiation shifts the dynamic from an arm-wrestling match into a joint engineering sprint. By separating personal relationships from the substantive dilemma, negotiators sit on the same side of the drafting table and treat the conflict as a shared design challenge.\n\nThe breakthrough lies in distinguishing stated positions from root interests. A position is what someone says they want; an interest is the quiet motivation driving that demand. Consider two chefs fighting over a single orange: positional bargaining splits the fruit in half, leaving both dissatisfied. Exploring interests reveals that one chef needs the peel for zest while the other needs the juice for a marinade. By inventing options before judging them, parties bake a larger pie instead of quarreling over crumbs.\n\nFinally, the framework removes intimidation through objective criteria and a robust walk-away option known as BATNA (Best Alternative to a Negotiated Agreement). Like a rock climber testing anchor points before shifting weight, you never rely on an opponent's goodwill. You anchor your terms to objective benchmarks\u2014such as fair market prices or standard safety protocols\u2014and secure a reliable fallback plan so you never feel coerced into a flawed deal.",
