      "headline": "Look for the Thirst, Not the Glass",
      "keyInsight": "Consider two home cooks arguing over the very last orange in the pantry fruit basket. Both demand the whole fruit. If they make a classic compromise, they take a kitchen knife and slice it straight down the middle. That seems fair on paper, right? But look closer at what happens next. The first cook squeezes their half for juice and tosses the peel into the compost. The second cook carefully grates the peel to flavor a cake and tosses the pulpy fruit into the trash. Half of the orange was completely wasted! By squabbling over the physical object rather than their underlying hunger, both settled for half of what they could have had. Stated demands hide the real appetite.",
      "content": "A demand is what somebody says they must have. An interest is the quiet reason why they want it in the first place. Think of an iceberg drifting in the cold ocean. The visible peak sticking out of the water is a rigid demand like \"I need ten thousand dollars tomorrow.\" The massive mountain resting under the waves contains basic human needs: security, recognition, peace of mind, and financial survival.\n\nWhen you only argue about the tip of the iceberg, your ship usually crashes. But when you ask curious questions about what lies beneath, everything shifts. Ask \"Why does that timeline matter to you?\" or \"What are you hoping this schedule protects?\" You will often discover that their underlying appetite does not conflict with yours at all.\n\nBehind every stubborn demand sits a collection of everyday human desires. People want to feel safe in their homes. They want their hard work recognized. Once you pinpoint the actual thirst driving their request, you can pour several different drinks that satisfy everyone at the table.",
