      "id": "getting-to-yes-negotiating-agreement-without-giving-in-ch-4",
      "order": 4,
      "chapterNumber": 4,
      "headline": "Bake a Bigger Pie Before Slicing It",
      "keyInsight": "Most people treat bargaining like splitting the last slice of pizza. When you grab more, I get less. That fixed mindset shrinks our brain power instantly. We immediately judge every idea before it even leaves our mouth. In reality, two cooks can walk into a kitchen wanting the same single orange. One person needs the juice for a drink. The other only needs the peel to bake a cake. If they fight over who gets the orange, they cut it in half. Both walk away annoyed with half of what they wanted! When you separate thinking up ideas from judging them, hidden trade-offs suddenly appear everywhere. You do not win by fighting over crumbs. You win by tossing fresh ingredients into the bowl.",
      "content": "Imagine you and your friend are planning a road trip across the country. You both hold one steering wheel and fight over which single highway to take. The car never even leaves the driveway. Most negotiations stall out for the exact same reason. People assume there is only one pie on the table, and they must fight to grab the biggest slice.\n\nHere is the curious quirk of the human brain under pressure: judgment freezes creativity. When someone tosses out a wild idea, our reflex is to shoot it down instantly. We think, \"That will never work!\" or \"That costs too much money!\" But inventing ideas and judging ideas require two completely different mental modes. Doing both at the same time is like slamming on the gas pedal and the brakes together. Your wheels just smoke, and the engine burns out.\n\nTo break the gridlock, set up an idea sandbox. Sit together and brainstorm without any judgment allowed. Write every wild thought on a whiteboard. Look for differences that actually complement each other. Maybe you love driving fast on highways, but your friend loves navigating scenic backroads. Maybe you care deeply about quiet mornings, while they care about vibrant dinner spots. When your tastes differ, you have the secret recipe for an easy trade.\n\nDo not jump straight to a final contract. Instead, test-drive options like trying on jackets at a store. You can slip on an option, see how it fits, and put it back without buying it yet. When both sides feel free to tinker, magic happens. You stop fighting over one small slice because you just baked an entire bakery together.",
