      "chapterNumber": 7,
      "headline": "Negotiation Jujitsu: Sidestep the Punch and Channel the Force",
      "keyInsight": "When someone pushes you hard, your automatic biological impulse is to shove back twice as hard. But pushing a brick wall only bruises your shoulders and locks both of you in place. Negotiation jujitsu flips this everyday physics equation on its head. Instead of resisting their fierce blow, you simply step aside and let their own momentum carry them forward. When they attack your idea, don't defend it. When they attack you personally, don't counterpunch. Treat every punch as a clumsy attempt to solve an underlying problem. If you don't push back, they have nothing to push against! By asking curious questions and using deliberate silence, you turn their stubborn battering ram into a team problem-solving session.",
      "content": "Imagine you are driving down a narrow mountain lane and encounter another car speeding straight at you. If you speed up to defend your patch of asphalt, you both end up in a metal crunch. But if you smoothly ease into a turnoff, the speeding car zooms harmlessly past empty air. In heated conversations, most people act like two stubborn drivers playing chicken. One person digs their heels in, so the other person brings a louder megaphone. Negotiation jujitsu is the art of giving aggressive energy nothing solid to collide with.\n\nThink about water rushing down a rocky creek bed. Water never picks a fistfight with a giant boulder. It doesn't scream or crack itself in half trying to shove the stone out of the way. Instead, it curves gracefully around the edges, letting gravity do the heavy lifting until it finds a natural groove. When a stubborn partner attacks your proposal, treat their jab as an invitation to investigate their underlying motive. Ask them, \"What concerns lead you to see it this way?\" Now their hostile energy is channeled into answering a puzzle rather than slamming a door.\n\nAnother secret tool in this toolbelt is pure, unvarnished quiet. Most people treat silence like an awkward hole they must rush to plug with nervous chatter. But in a tense chat, quiet functions like a shock absorber on a mountain bike. When they lay out an unfair ultimatum, say nothing at all. Just sit back, breathe calmly, and keep looking at them with open curiosity. The uncomfortable stillness invites them to keep talking, and within moments, they often begin softening their own impossible demands without you lifting a finger.",
